{
 "id": "2026-11-15-a",
 "title": "You've been told DNA is tiny. It's 2 metres 🧬",
 "description": "Each nucleus in your cells holds about 2 metres of DNA, packed into a space ~6 microns wide. Scale the nucleus up to a tennis ball and that's ~20 km of thread. It fits by winding around protein spools (histones) and coiling again and again. Add up the trillions of cells with a nucleus (red blood cells have none) and it's billions of km, farther than the Sun is from Neptune (~4.5 billion km).\n\nSources: BBC Science Focus; Sender, Fuchs & Milo 2016 (via Science News); OpenOregon Biology.",
